# Create forms must not disclose persisted private values

Status: security correction implemented; independent correction review pending.
This decision separates the security boundary from reference parity claims.

The independent signup review reproduced an anonymous named-new request that
returned an unreadable existing applicant's email. The route retained the
persisted card, the outer view checked create permission, and the internal
account form rendered stored field values without their read check. The same
serving engine denied reading that email before and after the request.

Require affirmative update permission as well as the existing create check
before rendering `new`, `new_in_modal`, `simple_new` or `new_fields` on a
persisted card. Missing update evidence refuses. Apply this before hooks and
dispatch, and name update in the focal 403 response. A rowless new target
retains its create-only gate. Existing trusted internal `skip_perms` semantics
are unchanged; no external request option is granted that capability.

The pinned Ruby source declares these views with `perms: :create`, permits
named read requests to fetch persisted records, and renders account formgroups
under bot scope. This additional update requirement is deliberate security
hardening, not asserted byte parity with that unsafe path. No executed Ruby
exploit has been established by this decision. Reference differences must be
retained and explained rather than normalized into matches.

The original disclosure, the failing regression and independently authored
counterexample remain evidence. The correction must preserve unnamed and named
rowless creation, outer create refusals and authorized existing editors.
Independent review also challenges public holders with private fields and
virtual/orphaned account children; a persisted-root fix alone is not evidence
that those additional paths are safe. Release acceptance remains withheld.
